```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks, Nadia. On my end, here's what's needed to resolve the runtime blocker. First, my immediate action item is to update the runtime sheet today to reflect the 52-minute cap from the archive-footage license — that should have been done already and I'll correct it right after this meeting. Second, to address the actual conflict, we have a few paths: we could negotiate an amendment with the archive-footage licensor to increase the permitted runtime, we could cut the documentary down to 52 minutes or under, or we could explore replacing the licensed archive footage with material that doesn't carry the same runtime restriction. Each path has different implications — an amendment would likely require a fee and turnaround time from the licensor, cutting to 52 minutes means losing 16 minutes of the assembly which is a significant creative decision that would need the director's involvement, and replacing footage means additional post-production time. I can reach out to the post house today to ask the archive licensor about amendment feasibility and timing if Friday is still the target. Third, I should note that any runtime change will ripple into Leon's cue sheet preparation, since he's drafting from the current 68-minute assembly — so we'll need to coordinate on a locked cut before either of us can finalize our deliverables. I'll get the runtime sheet updated right after this meeting and can initiate the licensor conversation today."}
```

